  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 246 AM CDT Tue Sep 22 2026\n\n500 analysis early this morning shows the beginning of a Rex Block \npattern with a ridge building across central Canada into Hudson Bay. \nThe location of the ridge will promote the development of high \npressure over the Great Lakes. A high pressure that will play an \nimportant role in precipitation chances into next week as dry air \nassociated with it will make it tough to get rain chances east of I-\n35.\n\nAlthough we have this ridge to our north, you can still see a nice \nspin on water vapor imagery near the Quad Cities and it is feature \nthat has helped keep clouds across far southern MN with the \noccasional spit of light rain. For the rest of today, where you see \nclouds this morning (southern MN, then up the Buffalo Ridge) will be \nwhere we continue to see the most cloud cover today, but we will at \nleast get to see more of the sun today that we've seen over the last \nfew days. By tonight, we will see mostly clear skies, with the \ncoolest night of the week expected, with lows dipping into the upper \n30s from the area of Mille Lacs over to northwest WI. Wednesday will \nsee increasing clouds in western MN, but that Great Lakes high will \nhold any precip back west in the Dakotas.\n\nWednesday night into Thursday will bring our next chance for rain. \nWhat is interesting about this rain chance is the NBM wind forecast \nshows no indication of a surface front moving through, with that \nfeature washing out in the Dakotas before reaching MN. However, the \nplume of increased moisture, with PWATs between 1.5\" and 1.75\", will \npush on without the front. It's this plume of deeper moisture (PWATs \nnear 180% of normal) that will allow for showers to move across MN \nWednesday night into Thursday morning. Coverage of showers will be \ngreatest in western MN (where PoPs are now in the 80s) that tail off \nto less than 20% by the time you get to WI. We think the 00z RRFS \nhas a good general idea of what to expect, with showers and \nthunderstorms developing over western MN Wednesday evening, that \nwill work east through the night, likely falling apart before \nreaching the I-35 corridor Thursday morning. Instability looks to be \nenough to drive a thunder threat, but not a severe weather threat.\n\nFriday will likely be another cloudy day, with plenty of mid and \nupper level moisture remaining, but there's no forcing precip, which \nmeans yet another day with temperatures staying in the 60s under \nmostly cloudy skies. Saturday will see a shortwave work across the \nDakotas into northwest Ontario. This will pull another plume of \nincreased moisture across MN Saturday into Saturday night, kind of \nsimilar to what we'll see Thursday. Saturday doesn't look like a \nwashout, though for MN, some scattered showers are looking \nincreasingly likely. The closer to WI you get on Saturday, the more \ninfluence you'll have from the dry high pressure over the Great \nLakes, which will again make life difficult for rain to push into \nthat drier airmass.\n\nAgain we dry out on Sunday before we see another attempt at a \nfrontal passage early next week. There's still a good deal of spread \nwith what will happen early next week. The GFS has continued with \nthe idea of stronger system and associated cold front working across \nthe area Monday night, but the ECMWF has backed off on this idea, \ninstead keeping the ridging and dry high pressure locked into the \nGreat Lakes.",
